  "text": "**Solution 3 has the correct answer, 1/2.** I checked the work by hand, without running code.\n\n**Lower bound g(n) ≥ n/2 holds.** The reduction to 3-AP-free subsets is valid, and each element is the middle of at most one 3-AP. Greedy from largest to smallest gives a 3-AP-free S. A skipped element x must be the left endpoint of a blocking AP {x, c, r} with center c in S. Two skipped elements with the same center would give x + r = x' + r' with distinct pairs, forcing x = x'. So skipped ≤ |S|, and h(A) ≥ n/2.\n\n**The construction is correct for t = 3.** The 9-point set {-15, -8.25, -6, -1.5, 0, 3, 4, 6, 8} has all 36 pairwise sums distinct, so it is weak Sidon. Its 3-APs are {a,b,d}, {a,c,f}, {c,d,f}, {c,e,h}, {e,f,h}, {e,g,i}, {g,h,i}. A case check shows no 3 vertices hit all seven, so τ = 4 and α = 5 = n/2 + 1/2. This also refutes both other lower bounds: Solution 1 needs α ≥ 3n/5 = 5.4, and Solution 2 needs α ≥ 2n/3 = 6.\n\n**Padding is valid.** Generic reals add no APs or sum collisions, so g(n) ≤ n/2 + O(1).\n\n**Caveat.** The chain claim α = t+2 for all t, meaning each gadget raises τ by exactly 1, is plausible but I did not verify it beyond t = 3. The limit 1/2 depends on it. The lower bound is solid, so the answer is at least 1/2, and the only consistent value that survives the counterexamples above is 1/2.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
